answer:  First, we need to determine how far Julia rowed in 64 seconds. Since it took her 16 seconds to row 20 feet, we can set up a proportion to find out how far she rowed in 64 seconds.

\[
\frac{16 \text{ seconds}}{20 \text{ feet}} = \frac{64 \text{ seconds}}{x \text{ feet}}
\]

Solving for \( x \):

\[
x = \frac{64 \text{ seconds}}{16 \text{ seconds}} \times 20 \text{ feet} = 4 \times 20 \text{ feet} = 80 \text{ feet}
\]

Next, we know that the boat takes on 2 liters of water for every 10 feet she rowed. Therefore, we need to find out how many liters of water the boat takes on for 80 feet.

\[
\frac{2 \text{ liters}}{10 \text{ feet}} = \frac{y \text{ liters}}{80 \text{ feet}}
\]

Solving for \( y \):

\[
y = \frac{2 \text{ liters}}{10 \text{ feet}} \times 80 \text{ feet} = 2 \times 8 \text{ liters} = 16 \text{ liters}
\]

Thus, the boat had taken on 16 liters of water by the time Julia reached shore.
