\[ \text{Volume} = \text{length} \times \text{width} \times \text{height} = 6 \, \text{inches} \times 6 \, \text{inches} \times 15 \, \text{inches} = 540 \, \text{cubic inches} \]

Next, calculate the number of jelly beans that can fit in the jar:
\[ \text{Number of jelly beans} = \frac{\text{Volume of the jar}}{\text{Volume of one jelly bean}} = \frac{540 \, \text{cubic inches}}{0.15 \, \text{cubic inches}} = 3600 \, \text{jelly beans} \]

Now, account for the packing efficiency:
\[ \text{Actual number of jelly beans} = \text{Number of jelly beans} \times \text{packing efficiency} = 3600 \, \text{jelly beans} \times 0.80 = 2880 \, \text{jelly beans} \]

Tim estimates 30% of the jelly beans are red:
\[ \text{Guessed number of red jelly beans} = \text{Actual number of jelly beans} \times \text{estimated percentage of red jelly beans} = 2880 \, \text{jelly beans} \times 0.30 = 864 \, \text{jelly beans} \]

The true number of red jelly beans is 900. To find out how far off Tim's guess was:
\[ \text{Difference} = \text{Guessed number of red jelly beans} - \text{True number of red jelly beans} = 864 \, \text{jelly beans} - 900 \, \text{jelly beans} = -36 \, \text{jelly beans} \]

Since the difference is negative, it means Tim's guess was too low. The absolute value of the difference is:
\[ \text{Absolute difference} = |-36 \, \text{jelly beans}| = 36 \, \text{jelly beans} \]

Therefore, Tim's guess was 36 jelly beans less than the true number of red jelly beans.

